## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical ability to diagnose multi-tenant feature-flag issues in production using Kubernetes, Redis, and tracing. It probes understanding of cache stampede, per-tenant routing, rollout safety, and latency requirements.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant feature flags and cache invalidation
- Canary rollouts and rollback safety
- Redis keyspace examination and TTL semantics
- Distributed tracing for tenant-aware requests
- Latency SLO verification under load

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo canary rollout flow
if (flagEnabledForTenant(tenant)) {
  routeToTenantService(tenant);
} else {
  routeToFallback(tenant);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for 10k tenants with dynamic flag sets?
- What rollback criteria would you codify (latency, error rate, traffic shift)?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests diagnosing certificate rotation within a service mesh, focusing on end-to-end TLS verification, per-tenant isolation, and zero-downtime deployment strategies.

## Key Concepts

- TLS certificate rotation propagation across Kubernetes secrets and Envoy proxies
- mTLS handshake troubleshooting in Istio service mesh
- Canary rollouts with automated rollback capabilities
- Per-tenant routing and isolation in multi-tenant mesh environments

## Code Example

```javascript
// TLS handshake tester (pseudo)
async function testTLS(target) {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(target);
    return response.ok;
  } catch {
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you monitor certificate expiration dates across all pods?
- What metrics would you use to detect TLS handshake failures in real-time?
- How do you ensure tenant isolation during certificate rotation?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests distributed-systems thinking: exactly-once, dedup, backpressure, observability.

## Key Concepts

- Exactly-once processing
- Deduplication with dedup_id
- Backpressure strategies (batching, pause vs drop)
- Observability (tracing, metrics, logs)
- Kafka vs Kinesis trade-offs
- Idempotent sinks and compensating actions

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode for idempotent sink write
function writeEvent(evt, sink, store) {
  const id = evt.dedup_id;
  if (store.seen(id)) return;
  sink.write(evt);
  store.markSeen(id);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test guarantees for partial failures?
- How would you ensure exactly-once with at-least-once queues?
